  "reasoning_content": "We need answer user's request: Explain how relational database executes SQL query, from parsing and planning through indexing, joins, transactions, returning rows. Detailed technical tutorial with concrete examples and tradeoffs, common performance pitfalls. Complete sentences, avoid repeating. Need final answer only. Need be thorough. Need structure. We can write tutorial with examples SQL, maybe PostgreSQL-ish, mention MVCC, locks, isolation levels, buffer cache, query optimizer stats, cost models, execution plans, index types B-tree/hash, covering, partial, composite, join algorithms nested loop/hash/merge, predicate pushdown, common subexpressions, CTEs, materialization, transactions ACID, concurrency, locks, snapshots, WAL, commit, isolation anomalies, MVCC vs locking, performance pitfalls: missing indexes, bad stats, correlation, fan-out, implicit casts, functions preventing index, OR predicates, NOT IN, LIMIT without order, deep pagination, large transactions, table scans, sequential scans not always bad, index bloat, fragmentation, over-indexing, deadlocks, lock contention, isolation levels, read uncommitted, long transactions, vacuum bloat, connection pooling. Need ensure complete sentences. Avoid repeating. Maybe include a concrete schema: customers, orders, order_items, products. Query example. Walk through: lexical analysis parse, AST, semantic analysis name resolution, catalog, authorization, rewriting (views, partitioning, CTE), optimization: enumerate plans, statistics, cost, choose plan, index scans, joins, sort/hash/aggregation, execution engine, buffer manager, index access, heap/table access, visibility checks, locks, WAL, commit. Mention physical: pages, buffers, I/O, caching. Transactions: begin, snapshot, locks on modified rows, WAL, checkpoint, commit metadata. Returning rows: executor produces tuples to client via protocol, may stream or buffer.\n\nNeed maybe include plan examples in text. Need include tradeoffs: index